s hyperglycemia and nutritional deficiencies. [Data: Entities (1641); Relationships (3273, 551, 6906)]\n\n## Nutritional management is critical for patient recovery\n\nNutritional support is essential in managing patients with acute pancreatitis, particularly through enteral and parenteral nutrition. Enteral nutrition is recommended for stable patients to enhance their nutritional status and reduce infection risks, while parenteral nutrition is crucial for those unable to tolerate oral feeding. This nutritional management is vital for improving recovery outcomes and preventing complications such as malnutrition. [Data: Entities (6839, 12254); Relationships (4611, 2641, 6756)]\n\n## Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS) as a complication\n\nSIRS can develop as a complication of severe acute pancreatitis, characterized by a systemic inflammatory response that complicates patient management. The inflammatory process can lead to a hypermetabolic state, necessitating careful nutritional management. Immunonutrition has been noted to mitigate SIRS duration in post-surgical patients, highlighting the interconnectedness of these conditions. [Data: Entities (2203); Relationships (2779, 6897)]\n\n## Multiple organ failure as a severe complication\n\nSevere acute pancreatitis can result in multiple organ failure, significantly increasing mortality risk. This condition arises when the inflammatory response affects various organ systems, necessitating comprehensive management strategies to monitor and support organ function. The risk of organ failure underscores the critical nature of acute pancreatitis and the need for intensive care. [Data: Entities (2310); Relationships (6913, 6896)]\n\n## Biliary lithiasis as a common cause of acute pancreatitis\n\nBiliary lithiasis, or gallstones, is recognized as a leading cause of acute pancreatitis, accounting for approximately 35% of episodes. The presence of gallstones can obstruct the pancreatic duct, leading to inflammation and serious complications. Understanding this relationship is crucial for effective diagnosis and management of acute pancreatitis. [Data: Entities (6852); Relationships (6894)]\n\n## C-reactive protein (CRP) as a marker of inflammation\n\nC-reactive protein (CRP) is a significant biomarker for assessing inflammation in patients with acute pancreatitis. Elevated CRP levels can indicate the severity of the condition and are particularly relevant in monitoring nutritional status and recovery. This makes CRP a valuable tool in the clinical management of acute pancreatitis. [Data: Entities (3079); Relationships (828, 6912)]\n\n## The role of diabetes mellitus in acute pancreatitis management\n\nDiabetes mellitus complicates the management of patients suffering from acute pancreatitis, particularly in terms of nutritional support and glycemic control. The interplay between these conditions necessitates careful monitoring and tailored treatment strategies to address both the pancreatitis and the underlying diabetes. [Data: Entities (2202); Relationships (1550)]\n\n## Alcohol consumption as a significant risk factor\n\nExcessive alcohol consumption is one of the primary etiologies of acute pancreatitis, contributing to pancreatic tissue damage. Understanding the role of alcohol in the development of this condition is essential for prevention and management strategies, particularly in at-risk populations. [Data: Entities (6853); Relationships (6905)]\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n590,VÓMITOS,\"VÓMITOS, or vomiting, is defined as the involuntary expulsion of the contents of the stomach through the mouth. This phenomenon can occur as a symptom of various underlying health issues, particularly in patients with gastrointestinal disorders. It is commonly observed in individuals suffering from conditions such as pancreatitis, cancer, and gastrointestinal disorders, including EPOC and gastroenteritis eosinofílica. \n\nVomiting can significantly impact a patient's nutritional status, leading to malnutrition if it occurs frequently. In particular, it complicates the administration of enteral nutrition, as it may indicate intolerance to feeding, especially in infants. Additionally, vomiting can influence the nutritional and hydration needs of children, complicating their management in parenteral nutrition settings. \n\nMoreover, vomiting is a notable side effect of chemotherapy, which can hinder a patient's ability to maintain proper nutrition. It can also result in fluid and electrolyte loss, further affecting overall health. In summary, VÓMITOS is a critical clinical sign that can arise from various medical conditions and has significant implications for a patient's nutritional and hydration status.\",11\r\n2343,NAÚSEAS,\"NAÚSEAS refers to the sensation of needing to vomit, which is a common symptom experienced by patients with cancer. This condition can significantly limit their intake of food, thereby impacting their overall nutritional health. Nausea is not only a distressing symptom but also complicates the administration of enteral nutrition, making it a critical concern in the management of patients undergoing treatment. As a side effect of various medications, nausea can further exacerbate the challenges faced by these patients in maintaining adequate nutrition.\",3\r\n6958,ICTUS ISQUÉMICO,\"Ictus isquémico refers to a type of stroke that occurs due to a blockage in blood flow to the brain. This interruption in circulation can result in potential brain damage, highlighting the critical nature of timely medical intervention in such cases.\",2\r\n6725,VARÓN,\"VARÓN is a 75-year-old male individual who has been admitted due to an ischemic stroke. He weighs 70 kg and has a height of 1.67 m. His energy expenditure is calculated using specific formulas, and he requires enteral nutrition to support his recovery.\",25\r\n6719,UCI,\"The Unidad de Cuidados Intensivos (UCI), also known as the Intensive 